  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Refining Client Needs**\n\nI now understand! This isn\'t building a CRM, but crafting an effective questionnaire for clients *seeking* a CRM. The goal is to elicit specific requirements from the client to provide the development team with a clear plan to fulfill the client needs, not just make something. I\'ll focus on how to best get the most helpful responses.\n\n\n**Clarifying Form Focus**\n\nI\'ve shifted my focus to the form\'s core purpose: gathering client perspectives on CRM needs. It\'s about a straightforward, line-by-line approach to elicit essential details directly from the client. There\'s an effort to eliminate unneeded values. I will aim to get the most information. It\'s a key function to get the most information from the client. The form aims to give clients the space to tell me what they want.\n\n\n**Designing CRM Customization**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how the form will allow clients to customize their CRM requirements. I\'m thinking about including auto-updates based on payment statuses and the ability to record subsidy status history, linked to specific projects with reasons for each status change. The client\'s bank info should be easily incorporated if needed. This also includes delete, edit and add buttons with an easy to navigate system. I will add sections and add points to the lines, add anyone in each and everyone section.\n\n\n**Structuring Form Sections**\n\nI\'m now outlining the form\'s structure, dividing it into sections like Customers and Projects. For each section, I\'ll include key fields, along with options to approve, delete, or toggle specific elements based on the client\'s needs, such as the branch and point of contact info. There\'s an \"Add new custom field\" button for each section, along with the ability to easily add and remove values in each section. I\'m focusing on simplicity and customization.\n\n\n**Organizing Financial Data**\n\nI\'m now detailing financial sections, emphasizing project stage inputs (3-15 stages allowed) and financial fields like amounts and payment types, with auto-calculations for automatic revisions based on entered payments. Clients can now toggle or add new payment methods. There is a section for subsidy status history, that the client may choose to have or not. There is also a section for client bank information. There are \"Add\", \"Delete\", and \"Approve\" buttons for each section.\n\n\n**Visualizing Form Layout**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the form\'s visual design and user experience. I\'m prioritizing a clean, high-contrast theme to appear professional and trustworthy. I\'m exploring a structured layout, like a stepper, with clear section divisions and line-by-line options. Each section will have a list of fields with toggles for inclusion/exclusion, inline notes, and an \"Add custom field\" button, to add to the customer\'s needs and wants. There will be an interactive live preview. At the very top, I\'ll have the client enter their contact information. They\'ll also be able to \"Submit Requirements\" or \"Export CRM Blueprint\". I will include the toggle questions for bank details and loan numbers.\n\n\n**Integrating API Assistance**\n\nI\'m now integrating the Gemini API to generate a custom CRM development proposal directly from form inputs! This is a pivotal step, transforming client choices into a detailed, ready-to-use plan. This will make it feel powerful, amazing, and highly functional. It\'s a huge value-add and a perfect fit for a client ordering a CRM.\n\n\n"
