  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Investigating App Behavior**\n\nI\'m now digging into `/src/App.tsx`\'s past versions to pinpoint when the \"Live CRM DB Schema\" or \"Dynamic Preview\" pane was rendered, now that it is known we shouldn\'t show it to clients. I need to understand when and if these were present.\n\n\n**Refining Interface Requirements**\n\nI\'ve got a clearer vision now. The client wants a super clean, light-themed interface built with HTML, CSS, and JS, leveraging Lucide icons for a crisp look. This isn\'t about building a CRM, it\'s about a form clients can use to specify their CRM needs. It also needs to be simple, no fakes, so I have removed \"Live CRM DB Schema\" or \"Dynamic Preview\" pane was rendered. The admin side must be password-protected, with the password fixed during setup.\n\n\n**Implementing Password Protection**\n\nI\'m now focusing on securing the admin view. My plan involves a hardcoded password check, prompting the user with a clean modal or inline input field like \"Enter Developer Passcode to view submissions.\" I\'m thinking of a constant like \"solar-admin-2026\" or something customizable, with an option for the user to hardcode their own. I also need to make sure project stages and fin values are empty options, so the user can fill them as needed.\n\n\n**Clarifying Form Input Fields**\n\nI\'m now making sure the questionnaire form design reflects the user\'s input preferences. No pre-filled stages or financial tags will be displayed. Instead, the user will get empty input fields to add their own stages or financial tags, one by one. I\'m also including \"I don\'t know / skip\" options. This design aims for flexibility and a user-driven experience, and I\'m very happy with how it\'s shaping up.\n\n\n**Designing Form Header & Fields**\n\nI\'m now structuring the interface, starting with a \"Solar CRM Specification Portal\" header, featuring the Sun icon. I am now working on the client information section. This section focuses on the installer\'s client, like name, phone, and email, to enable contact. After that, I am designing the Customers Module, including fields like `name`, `phone`, and `email`, and others. Then I am planning some conditional features, such as removing the `branch` field if the user only has one. I am showing \"Approve\" and \"Remove\" options. Finally, I will also implement custom fields. I am looking forward to the next part.\n\n\n**Defining Project Components**\n\nNow, I\'m fleshing out the form\'s core sections: Project Stages and Financials. For Project Stages, I\'ll provide a blank slate for users to define their own stages, allowing between 3 and 15, or the option to skip. In the Financials Module, I\'m including fields like quoted amount, received, receivable, and discount, letting users customize payment types. I\'ll explain that the calculations are dynamic. I\'m also considering an optional subsidy status history section with its own specific fields. This should be an effective and flexible design.\n\n\n**Adding Optional Sections**\n\nI\'m now integrating new, optional sections into the form. I\'ve designed a \"Government Subsidies\" section with `status` (rejected/approved/pending), `date`, and `reason` fields, controlled by a simple toggle. Also, I am adding a **Bank & Financing Info** section with fields like `account_name`, `bank_name`, and `loan_app_number` that users can enable/disable with another toggle. I\'m focusing on user control and a streamlined interface. Next, I\'ll be implementing the comments section and submit button.\n\n\n**Implementing Password Persistence**\n\nI\'ve realized a brilliant approach to password management, perfectly capturing the \"once I set the password it would be that password\" requirement. I\'ll check `localStorage` for a password. If none exists, the user sets it on first use. Otherwise, they use the saved password. I am going to save the password in `localStorage.setItem(\'solar_admin_password\')`. I\'m happy with this solution.\n\n\n"
